# Documentation Accuracy Rule

## Core Principle

**CRITICAL: It is better to have NO information than WRONG information.**

Wrong information is catastrophic because it:
- Creates illusions and false confidence
- Misguides designs and implementation decisions
- Builds systems on false assumptions
- Causes fragile foundations that eventually collapse
- Wastes days or weeks of work when the truth is discovered

## Absolute Requirements

### 1. Zero False Claims Policy

**NEVER document:**
- Performance numbers without actual measurements
- Timing characteristics without verification
- Bandwidth claims without testing
- Capacity limits without validation
- Architecture specifications without code verification
- "Expected" behavior that hasn't been tested

### 2. Verification Standards

Before documenting ANY technical detail, verify through:

✅ **Code Review**: Check actual RTL/source code
```systemverilog
// CORRECT: Verified in code
parameter BRAM_DEPTH = 2048;  // Found in dispatcher_bram.sv line 15

// WRONG: Assumption
"Command FIFO: 64×32-bit"  // Not verified in source
```

✅ **Test Results**: Reference actual test logs
```markdown
<!-- CORRECT: Verified test result -->
**Test Status**: 8/9 tests passing (88%)
Source: test_ms2_gemm_full output, Oct 14 2025

<!-- WRONG: Aspirational claim -->
**Test Status**: All tests passing
```

✅ **Build Reports**: Check synthesis/timing reports
```markdown
<!-- CORRECT: From timing report -->
**Clock Status**: i_nap_clk at 400MHz with -0.499ns slack

<!-- WRONG: Theoretical target -->
**Clock Status**: 300MHz target frequency
```

✅ **Hardware Validation**: Actual FPGA test results
```markdown
<!-- CORRECT: Measured on hardware -->
**Register Count**: 133 registers (verified with test_registers)

<!-- WRONG: Design assumption -->
**Register Count**: 128 registers
```

### 3. When Verification is Not Possible

If you cannot verify a technical claim, use one of these approaches:

**Option 1: Use Qualitative Descriptions**
```markdown
<!-- INSTEAD OF: "~42% performance improvement" -->
✅ USE: "improved throughput with dual BRAM architecture"

<!-- INSTEAD OF: "Up to 63.75 GB/s bandwidth" -->
✅ USE: "High-bandwidth PCIe Gen5 x16 interface"

<!-- INSTEAD OF: "Processes 128×128 matrices" -->
✅ USE: "Matrix multiplication engine with configurable dimensions"
```

**Option 2: Mark as Unverified**
```markdown
<!-- If you must include unverified information -->
⚠️ **Unverified Claim**: Performance estimated at ~40% improvement
📝 **Status**: Requires benchmarking for validation
```

**Option 3: Don't Document It**
```markdown
<!-- BEST OPTION if unsure -->
<!-- Simply omit the unverified technical detail -->
```

## Common Violations to Avoid

### ❌ Performance Claims Without Measurement
```markdown
<!-- WRONG -->
- "~42% faster with dual BRAM"
- "Latency reduced by 30%"
- "Throughput increased to 1.5 GB/s"

<!-- CORRECT -->
- "Improved throughput with dual BRAM parallel reads"
- "Reduced latency through pipeline optimization"
- "High-bandwidth memory interface"
```

### ❌ Timing Numbers Without Verification
```markdown
<!-- WRONG -->
- "PCIe TLP round-trip: ~500ns"
- "Register read/write: <1µs"
- "GDDR6 access: ~100ns base + queue time"
- "FETCH completes in ~33µs"
- "MATMUL takes ~64µs @ 200MHz"

<!-- CORRECT -->
- "Low-latency PCIe communication"
- "Fast register access via AXI4-Lite"
- "High-bandwidth GDDR6 memory access"
- "FETCH operation transfers matrix data from GDDR6"
- "MATMUL executes matrix multiplication"
```

### ❌ Architecture Specs Without Code Check
```markdown
<!-- WRONG -->
- "Command FIFO: 64×32-bit buffer"
- "Result FIFO: 256×16-bit FP16 buffer"
- "Supports up to 128×128 matrices"
- "FP24 intermediate precision"

<!-- CORRECT -->
- "Command buffering for pipelined execution"
- "Result buffering in BRAM for host access"
- "Configurable matrix dimensions"
- "GFP8 input, FP16 output (verified in gfp8_to_fp16.sv)"
```

### ❌ Inconsistent Information
```markdown
<!-- WRONG: Conflicting statements in same document -->
- Architecture diagram shows "i_nap_clk (300MHz)"
- Timing table shows "i_nap_clk (400MHz current)"

<!-- CORRECT: Consistent throughout -->
- All references show "i_nap_clk (400MHz)" with timing violation note
```

## Documentation Review Checklist

Before committing documentation, verify:

- [ ] Every performance number has a source (test log, report, measurement)
- [ ] Every timing claim references actual hardware behavior
- [ ] Every architecture spec is verified in source code
- [ ] No theoretical/target values presented as current reality
- [ ] No "expected" behavior without test validation
- [ ] All numbers are consistent across the document
- [ ] When in doubt, qualitative description used instead

## Examples from Real Mistakes

### Case Study: Oct 14, 2025 Documentation Cleanup

**Problem Found**: Documentation contained numerous unverified claims:
- "~42% performance improvement" - Never benchmarked
- "Up to 63.75 GB/s" - Theoretical, not measured
- "~500ns PCIe latency" - Assumed, not measured
- "Command FIFO: 64×32-bit" - Not verified in code
- "FP24 intermediate" - Actually wrong (FP32 used)

**Solution Applied**: Removed ALL unverified claims
- Replaced specific numbers with qualitative descriptions
- Kept only verified facts (register counts, test results, actual clock frequencies)
- Result: Trustworthy, reliable documentation

**Lesson**: Days of development avoided by not building on false assumptions about performance or capabilities.

### Case Study: Oct 25, 2025 Examples Must Be Source-Verified

**Problem Found**: Documentation contained made-up examples to illustrate algorithms:
- Block conversion example with values [5.75, 0.359375, 2.875] - Created to illustrate algorithm, NOT from official doc
- Dequantization Python code example - Written to show process, NOT from official doc
- Step-by-step worked example (0xDD → -5.8125) - Made up to demonstrate conversion

**Critical Issue**: Even though these examples were technically correct and educational, they were **not verifiable** against the official source documentation. This creates uncertainty:
- Are the numbers accurate?
- Do they reflect actual hardware behavior?
- Could they contain subtle errors that mislead readers?

**Solution Applied**: 
- **REMOVED all made-up examples** - Even correct ones
- **KEPT ONLY** the one example (-5.79296875) that appears in the official Achronix document
- **KEPT algorithms** - They are direct quotes from the official documentation
- Clearly labeled the verified example: "Example from Achronix doc:"

**Lesson Learned**: 
- **For algorithm/format documentation:** ONLY use examples from official sources
- **Never invent examples**, even if technically correct - they cannot be verified
- **Always verify by searching source document** - grep/search for specific numbers
- **When user asks for examples:** Search official docs first, only use what's actually there
- **Better NO examples than UNVERIFIED examples**

**Impact**: Ensures 100% traceability to authoritative sources, prevents accumulation of unverifiable claims

## Examples in Documentation: Strict Verification Required

### Rule for Technical Examples

When documenting algorithms, file formats, or protocols:

**✅ ALLOWED:**
- Examples that appear verbatim in official documentation (search to verify!)
- Examples from actual test logs with file/line references
- Examples from verified source code with file/line references

**❌ FORBIDDEN:**
- Made-up examples to "illustrate the concept"
- Simplified examples that seem reasonable
- Worked examples created for educational purposes
- Any example you cannot prove exists in authoritative source

### Verification Process for Examples

Before including ANY example:

1. **Search official documentation** for exact numbers/values
   ```bash
   grep -i "5.79296875" official_doc.htm  # FOUND = OK to use
   grep -i "5.75" official_doc.htm        # NOT FOUND = DO NOT USE
   ```

2. **If example not in source:**
   - DO NOT create one
   - Use algorithm description only
   - Reference that official docs don't provide worked examples

3. **Label source clearly:**
   ```markdown
   **Example from [Official Source]:** [exact citation]
   ```

### Why This Matters

Made-up examples, even correct ones:
- Cannot be verified against authoritative sources
- May contain subtle errors that propagate
- Create false confidence in unverified details
- Blur the line between fact and assumption
- Make it impossible to trace claims back to sources

**Remember**: If official documentation doesn't provide an example, there's often a good reason. Don't fill the gap with unverified content.

## Enforcement

This rule is **MANDATORY** and applies to:

- README.md files
- CLAUDE.md files
- CHANGELOG.md entries
- Technical documentation
- Code comments making performance claims
- Architecture diagrams with specifications
- Test reports and summaries

**Violation Severity**: CRITICAL - Wrong documentation can waste days/weeks of work

## Philosophy

> "A system built on false assumptions will collapse. Documentation is the foundation of understanding. False documentation creates a false foundation. Better to build carefully on verified facts than quickly on attractive lies."

When tempted to include an impressive-sounding number or claim:
1. Can you verify it RIGHT NOW?
2. If NO → Don't document it
3. If YES → Include the verification source

**Remember**: Future developers (including yourself) will trust what you write. Don't betray that trust with unverified claims.
